erately disfavored."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of granulomatous disease manifestations (e.g., hilar lymphadenopathy, non-caseating granulomas) or other systemic inflammatory markers in the case text make this branch less plausibl…", "summary": "No findings of granulomatous disease (e.g., sarcoidosis) reduce B4's plausibility. B1 remains the leading hypothesis."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Malignancy with Hypercalcemia? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "summary": "No single finding definitively contradicts malignancy. Mildly reduced evidence for B1 but no major disconfirming features."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.882], ["B1.1", 0.392], ["B1.2", 0.294]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading branch (B1) has posterior 0.882 (>0.75) but unresolved dangerous children (B1.1, B1.2, B1.3 with danger ≥0.7) remain. No info_exhaustion trigger as PTH testing and imaging have not been ordered. Tree depth (level 2) is below max (3)."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.85, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.3918500622237978, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely explanation of this patient's current presentation?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Antacid overuse"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Increased 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Increased parathyroid hormone"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Malignancy"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Viral illness"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Hypervalinemia'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.882}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.1", "label": "Hematologic Malignancy (e.g., Multiple Myeloma)", "posterior": 0.392, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1.2", "B1.3", "B3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=25%, source=dxs]\n\n[RAG context for 'A 45-year-old African American woman presents to her primary care physician for not feeling well' (score=0.81):]\n  Source: Morphological and Immunophenotypic Clues to the WHO Categories of Acute Myeloid Leukaemia. > Coordination of Care\n  AML is a common hematological malignancy in adults. Despite many advances, the malignancy still carries a poor prognosis. Hence, it is best managed by a multidisciplinary team that includes a hematolo\n[RAG context for 'A 45-year-old African American woman presents to her primary care physician for not feeling well' (score=0.83):]\n  Source: Survey of 1264 patients with orbital tumors and simulating lesions: The 2002 Montgomery Lecture, part 1. > Deterrence and Patient Education\n  The management of tumors in an area as important as the orbit can be quite complicated. A thorough discussion is necessary to inform the patient of the potential causes, treatments, adverse events, an\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.87):]\n  Source: Classification of Cough as a Symptom in Adults and Management Algorithms: CHEST Guideline and Expert Panel Report. > Etiology\n  Tumors in the respiratory system can irritate airways and lead to a persistent cough. Cough is a common symptom among patients with lung cancer (57%) and is often a presenting symptom that can signifi\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.89):]\n  Source: Infectious and Inflammatory Pathways to Cough. > Assessment\n  Otherwise, a systemic approach should be used to identify any coexisting illness which may be the origin or compounding factor to a cough. Interestingly, one study in 1996 found that the character and\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  abdominal pain and trouble focusing → paraneoplastic syndrome → Paraneoplastic Hypercalcemia from Non-Hematologic Cancers\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for hypercalcemia via serum calcium and PTHrP levels; consider imaging for squamous cell carcinoma sources\n  weight loss and poor GERD control → gastrointestinal neoplasm → Solid Tumor with Bone Metastases\n    (frequency: rare)\n    Suggestion: Perform abdominal imaging and consider endoscopic evaluation to rule out primary gastrointestinal tumors\n  antacid overuse with cognitive symptoms → nutrient deficiency → Medication Overuse (Antacid-related)\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Check serum B12 and intrinsic factor antibodies to confirm deficiency as the mechanism\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
